Hard link vs. soft link


Part A - Create the links
Create a directory named link_lab and navigate to it.
mkdir link_lab
cd link_lab
pwd

Create a file named security.txt containing the following text:
cat > security.txt
Linux File System Security Lab Confidential Security Report
Create:
a hard link named security_hard.txt
ln security.txt security_hard.txt

a symbolic link named security_soft.txt
ln -s security.txt security_soft.txt

List all three files using a command that displays their inode numbers.
ls -li

Record the inode numbers of:
security.txt: 1710576
security_hard.txt: 1710576
security_soft.txt: 1710577
What difference do you observe?
security.txt and security_hard.txt have the same inode number, while security_soft.txt has a different inode number. This is because a hard link refers to the same inode as the original file, whereas a symbolic link is a separate file containing the target pathname.
 
Part B - Examine File Metadata

Use ls -l to examine the three files. Identify the file type of each file from the first character of the permission field.

ls -l 
security.txt: Regular file
security_hard.txt: Regular file security_soft.txt: Symbolic link

Compare the link count of the original file, hard link, and symbolic link. Explain the observed values.
ls -li

security.txt: link count 2
security_hard.txt: link count 2 security_soft.txt: link count 1
security.txt and security_hard.txt are two directory entries referring to the same inode, so the inode's link count is 2. The symbolic link is a separate inode, so its own link count is 1.
Use the stat command on all three files and record the information:
