Compare the file sizes of the original file, hard link, and symbolic link. Why might the size of the symbolic link differ from the original file?
The original file and hard link have the same file size because they refer to the same underlying inode and data. The symbolic link has a different, smaller size because it is a separate file that stores the pathname of the target, such as security.txt, rather than storing the actual file contents.


Part C - Investigate readlink
Execute readlink on security_soft.txt. What information is displayed?
readlink security_soft.txt

readlink displays the pathname stored inside the symbolic link. In this case, the symbolic link stores security.txt as its target.
Execute readlink on security_hard.txt. What do you observe?
readlink security_hard.txt

No output is displayed because security_hard.txt is a hard link, not a symbolic link. A hard link does not store a target pathname, it directly refers to the same inode as the original file.
Based on your observations, explain why readlink works differently for hard links and symbolic links.
A symbolic link is a separate file that stores a pathname pointing to another file, so readlink can display that pathname. A hard link does not store a pathname to another file, it is another directory entry for the same inode. Therefore, readlink produces no output for a hard link.
Use:
readlink -f security_soft.txt

What does the -f option reveal?
The -f option resolves the symbolic link and displays the absolute path of the final target file.
 
Part D - Content Modification
Append the following line to security.txt:
Unauthorized login detected
echo “Unauthorized login detected” >> security.txt 

Display the contents using all three filenames.
cat security.txt
cat security_hard.txt
cat security_soft.txt

Is the newly added content visible through both links? Explain why.
Yes, the newly added content is visible through both the hard link and the symbolic link. The hard link and security.txt refer to the same inode, while the symbolic link points to security.txt. Therefore, both links access the updated file contents.
Modify the file using security_hard.txt. Check the contents through security.txt and security_soft.txt. What do you observe?
echo “Security investigation started” >>security_hard.txt
cat security.txt
cat security_soft.txt

The modification is visible through both security.txt and security_soft.txt. This is because security_hard.txt and security.txt refer to the same inode, and security_soft.txt points to security.txt.
 
Part E - Rename Experiment
Rename security.txt to incident.txt
mv security.txt incident.txt
ls -li 

Try accessing the content through security_hard.txt. Does it still work?
 cat security_hard.txt

Yes. The hard link still works after the original file is renamed because both incident.txt and security_hard.txt refer to the same inode.
Try accessing the content through security_soft.txt. Does it still work?
cat security_soft.txt
No. The symbolic link does not work because it still points to security.txt, which no longer exists after the rename.
Use readlink security_soft.txt after renaming the original file. What path is still stored in the symbolic link?
readlink security_soft.txt

The symbolic link still stores the pathname security.txt, even though the actual file has been renamed to incident.txt.
Explain why renaming the original file affects the symbolic link differently from the hard link.
Renaming the original file changes its directory name but does not change its inode. Therefore, the hard link continues to access the same inode and data. A symbolic link stores the target pathname rather than the inode. Since it still contains security.txt after the file was renamed to incident.txt, the symbolic link becomes broken.
 
