No, it cannot be accessed
cat security_soft.txt 
Use ls -l and readlink to investigate the symbolic link after deleting its target.
ls -l
readlink security_soft.txt 

What is a dangling/broken symbolic link?
A dangling or broken symbolic link is a symbolic link whose target file or directory no longer exists. The link remains, but it cannot access the target.
Does deleting the original filename immediately delete the underlying data when a hard link exists? Justify your answer using the link count and inode.
Before removing incident.txt

ls -li 

After removing incident.txt
ls -li

No. In our experiment, incident.txt and security_hard.txt initially had the same inode 1710576 and a link count of 2. After deleting incident.txt, security_hard.txt still had inode 1710576, but the link count decreased to 1. Therefore, the underlying data was not deleted because the hard link still referred to the inode.


Part G - Directory and Filesystem Behaviour
Create a directory named evidence. Attempt to create a hard link to this directory. Record the result.
mkdir evidence
ln evidence_hard 

The operation failed because Linux generally does not allow users to create hard links to directories.
Create a symbolic link named evidence_link pointing to the evidence directory. Was the operation successful?
ln -s evidence_link
ls -l 

Yes, the operation was successful. A symbolic link can point to a directory.
 
Based on your experiment, compare hard and symbolic links with respect to directory linking.
Hard links to directories are generally not allowed because they could create directory cycles and complicate filesystem management. Symbolic links can point to directories because they simply store the target pathname.
